.Dd September 28, 2026
.Dt POLL 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm poll ,
.Nm ppoll
.Nd wait for descriptor readiness
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Fd #define _GNU_SOURCE
.In poll.h
.Ft int
.Fn poll "struct pollfd *fds" "nfds_t nfds" "int timeout"
.Ft int
.Fn ppoll "struct pollfd *fds" "nfds_t nfds" "const struct timespec *timeout" "const sigset_t *sigmask"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn poll
examines the
.Fa nfds
entries in
.Fa fds
and waits for readiness, a signal, or the timeout.
At most 1024 entries are accepted.
Each entry has the following fields:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct pollfd {
        int fd;
        short events;
        short revents;
};
.Ed
.Pp
.Va events
specifies the requested conditions;
.Va revents
receives the conditions found.
A negative descriptor is ignored and receives zero in
.Va revents .
A closed or out-of-range nonnegative descriptor, or one opened with
.Dv O_PATH ,
receives
.Dv POLLNVAL .
.Bl -tag -width POLLNVAL
.It Dv POLLIN
Input is available, or a stream has reached end of input.
A listening socket is readable when a connection is queued for acceptance.
.It Dv POLLOUT
Output can proceed without waiting.
.It Dv POLLPRI
Accepted, but no descriptor reports this condition.
.It Dv POLLERR
An error condition exists.
.It Dv POLLHUP
A peer has closed or hung up.
.It Dv POLLNVAL
The descriptor is invalid or was opened with
.Dv O_PATH .
.El
.Pp
.Dv POLLERR ,
.Dv POLLHUP ,
and
.Dv POLLNVAL
are reported even when not requested.
No other event bits are reported.
.Pp
Regular files and directories report requested read and write readiness
immediately.
Pipes report readiness from their buffered data, free space, and peer lifetime.
An epoll set is readable while a wait on it would report an event, and a
poll of it is woken by a change in any descriptor it watches, as
.Xr epoll 2
describes.
Terminals report input when the line discipline has data ready;
pseudoterminals also report peer hangup.
Console readiness is restricted to the descriptor's read and write modes.
.Pp
Datagram and protocol-bearing raw sockets are readable when a packet is
queued and are always writable.
A connected stream is readable with buffered data or a received FIN.
It is writable in the established or close-wait state while its send
buffer has space.
A pending connection error reports
.Dv POLLERR
and requested
.Dv POLLOUT .
A closed stream reports
.Dv POLLHUP .
An ioctl-only socket reports
.Dv POLLERR
immediately.
.Pp
Files under
.Pa /proc
report read readiness even at end of file.
Character devices, the framebuffer, and the mouse report requested read
and write readiness immediately.
Mouse readiness does not indicate that a packet is available.
.Pp
For
.Fn poll ,
.Fa timeout
is a number of milliseconds.
A negative value waits indefinitely; zero performs one scan without waiting.
A positive timeout
ends at the first 10 ms timer tick at or after its end, measured from
where in the tick in progress the call is, so the timeout never expires
before the time asked and at most a tick after it.
With no entries, a finite timeout acts as a sleep.
.Pp
.Fn ppoll
takes a relative timeout in seconds and nanoseconds.
A null timeout waits indefinitely; a zero timeout performs one scan.
Seconds must be nonnegative and nanoseconds must be in the range
0 through 999999999.
The timeout ends at a tick, as for
.Fn poll ,
and is not written back.
The kernel limits the seconds field to 21474836, keeps the nanoseconds,
and limits the whole ticks to 2147483646, about 248.55 days.
.Pp
A non-null
.Fa sigmask
replaces the signal mask for the wait, atomically with its start.
When a signal ends the wait, its handler runs under
.Fa sigmask
and the previous mask comes back when the handler returns; otherwise the
previous mask comes back as the call returns.
If no entry is ready, a pending signal that is neither blocked nor
ignored gives
.Er EINTR ;
.Dv SA_RESTART
does not restart the call, as on Linux.
.Pp
The i386 system call numbers are 168 for
.Nm poll
and 309 for
.Nm ppoll .
The latter takes a timespec with 32-bit fields.
Musl uses it while the seconds fit in 32 bits.
For a larger timeout, musl first tries
.Nm ppoll_time64
(414), which this kernel answers with
.Er ENOSYS ,
then falls back to 309 with clamped seconds.
.Pp
On x86-64
.Nm poll
is call 7 and
.Nm ppoll
271, whose timespec has 64-bit fields; there is no
.Nm ppoll_time64 .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
On success, these functions return the number of entries with nonzero
.Va revents ,
or zero when the timeout expires.
An invalid descriptor contributes to the count; it does not fail the call.
On failure they return -1 and set
.Va errno .
The raw kernel calls return a negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/pollprobe.c
polls two empty pipes with a 30 ms timeout and expects zero.
After writing one byte to one pipe, it expects one ready entry with
.Dv POLLIN .
Closing the other pipe's writer produces
.Dv POLLHUP
on its reader.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width ENOMEM
.It Er EFAULT
The descriptor array cannot be read or written, or the
.Fn ppoll
timeout cannot be read.
.It Er EINTR
No entry was ready and a signal that is neither blocked nor ignored was
pending.
.It Er EINVAL
.Fa nfds
exceeds 1024, or the
.Fn ppoll
timeout has invalid seconds or nanoseconds.
Linux also refuses an
.Fa nfds
above the caller's soft
.Dv RLIMIT_NOFILE ;
this kernel does not, so a process whose limit is 0 still polls the
descriptors it has, as OpenSSH's rlimit sandbox needs.
.It Er ENOMEM
The kernel could not allocate the descriptor or wait arrays.
.It Er ESRCH
There is no current process.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pipe 2 ,
.Xr read 2 ,
.Xr select 2 ,
.Xr socket 2 ,
.Xr write 2
